Subject: [PATCH] a revived bot at critical health has no self-preservation until it is too late

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

infra's wow-dev family (Grug, Ugga, Grog, Bork, Og) was found live tonight in
an active death loop: 34-36 deaths per character in one hour, all to the same
creature (a Flamescale Wyrmkin, well above their level), almost all at the
identical position. This is not the wander-drift bug patch 0006 already
fixed - 0006 stops a HEALTHY bot from wandering into danger. This is what
happens once a bot is ALREADY dead next to something lethal.

CONFIRMED LIVE, NOT GUESSED. Polling `overseer_snapshot` (the 5-second live
presence table, not the periodic character-save snapshot) every 4 seconds
during an active death showed exactly this sequence for one bot:

    health=0  in_combat=0  pos=(-7929.6,-1353.2)   <- just died
    health=1  in_combat=0  pos=(-7923.6,-1353.2)   <- revived
    health=1  in_combat=0  pos=(-7923.6,-1353.2)   <- 5s later, unmoved
    health=1  in_combat=0  pos=(-7923.6,-1353.2)   <- 10s later, unmoved
    health=1  in_combat=0  pos=(-7923.6,-1353.2)   <- 15s later, unmoved
    health=1  in_combat=0  pos=(-7923.6,-1353.2)   <- 20s later, unmoved

Alive, at 1 of 503 health, out of combat, motionless, for the entire
observation window - until the same creature returns and kills it again.

TWO GAPS, BOTH REQUIRED TO ACTUALLY BREAK THE LOOP:

1. GrindTargetValue::FindTargetForGrinding() (src/Ai/Base/Value/
   GrindTargetValue.cpp) checks the bot's `attackers` list FIRST, before the
   `possible targets` list, and returns any living attacker completely
   unfiltered - no level-gap check, no elite check - while the exact same
   two checks already exist a few lines below for every other candidate.
   The moment the Wyrmkin re-notices a bot standing on top of it, this is
   why the bot's own AI chooses to keep fighting it rather than disengage.

2. DeadStrategy.cpp routes a dead bot through "find corpse" / "revive from
   corpse", and AiFactory::AddDefaultNonCombatStrategies - the strategy set
   that governs a bot the instant it is alive again but not yet back in
   combat - never adds "flee" at all (confirmed by reading the function; it
   is added only inside AddDefaultCombatStrategies). FleeStrategy's existing
   "critical health" -> "flee" trigger (FleeStrategy.cpp) is real, but
   FleeAction needs AI_VALUE(Unit*, "current target"), which only combat
   target-selection ever sets - it is empty for a bot that just revived out
   of combat, so even where "flee" happens to be loaded, this specific
   trigger's action is a guaranteed no-op at the one moment it matters most.

WHY A NEW ACTION RATHER THAN REUSING FleeAction

FleeManager (Mgr/Move/FleeManager.h), which FleeAction itself calls
internally, does not actually need a target unit - it takes a `Player* bot`
and a distance, and scores candidate destination points by distance from
nearby creatures; the `followAngle` constructor argument is marked
`[[maybe_unused]]` in FleeManager's own header. FleeAction only requires a
target because it computes ITS OWN angle from `target->GetAngle(bot)` before
calling FleeManager - that computation is what breaks with no target, not
FleeManager itself. RetreatFromDangerAction calls FleeManager directly with
a placeholder angle, which FleeManager's own destination search ignores, so
it works with no target at all.

THE FIX, IN TWO PARTS

- GrindTargetValue.cpp: the attackers loop gets the same level-gap (>4,
  non-player) and elite (rank > CREATURE_ELITE_NORMAL, no "can fight elite")
  checks already applied to `possible targets`, so a bot no longer commits to
  fighting back against something that already proved lethal.
- A new RetreatFromDangerAction (MovementActions.h/.cpp), registered in
  ActionContext.h as "retreat from danger", wired to the EXISTING "critical
  health" trigger in FleeStrategy.cpp, and "flee" added to the non-combat
  engine in AiFactory.cpp (class-agnostic - added once, before the per-class
  switch, since critical-health self-preservation is not a class feature).
  This is the action that actually runs the moment a bot is alive again at
  critical health, regardless of whether combat has resumed yet.

Neither half alone is sufficient: the grind-target gate stops the bot from
VOLUNTEERING to re-engage, but does nothing to physically move it away from
a spot the creature will simply re-notice it at; the retreat action moves the
bot away, but without the grind-target gate a bot could still choose to walk
back and re-engage on its own initiative once healed.

NOT ADDRESSED BY THIS PATCH: the underlying question of whether "revive from
corpse" (self-resurrect at the death location) or auto-release-to-graveyard
is the right default for a group without a real player master - that is a
separate design question this patch deliberately does not touch. This patch
only ensures that WHEREVER a bot ends up after reviving, being at critical
health and out of combat is not a state it can be stuck motionless in.

ROUND 2, 2026-08-28: DEPLOYED AND STILL DID NOT WORK - REAL FIX BELOW

Both halves above compiled, deployed, and were confirmed running the correct
binary - and the death loop continued unchanged (11 more deaths in 5 minutes
on a freshly-restarted worldserver). Static re-reading of the whole chain
found nothing wrong on paper, so a temporary diagnostic patch (deleted in
this same commit) added stderr logging at four points. Live evidence:
AddDefaultNonCombatStrategies fired correctly for all five family members
(the strategy loads); RetreatFromDangerAction::Execute fired correctly for
OTHER bots elsewhere in the world - but never once, in any log, for Grug,
Ugga, Grog, Bork, or Og.

THE ACTUAL BUG: Engine::ProcessTriggers has a second gate, separate from
action priority entirely: `if (minimal && node->getFirstRelevance() < 100)
continue;`. PlayerbotAI::UpdateAI passes minimal=true whenever
AllowActivity() is false - and AllowActivity() is false for any bot with no
real player in its zone/map/radius/guild and no real-player group leader
(confirmed: AiPlayerbot.BotActiveAlone=10 in this deployment, meaning only
~10% of ticks for an unwatched bot run at full activity). The family is
isolated in Burning Steppes with no real player anywhere near them, so they
are in "minimal" mode the large majority of the time. ACTION_EMERGENCY + 8 =
98, strictly less than 100 - the retreat trigger's handler was silently
dropped on every minimal tick, regardless of its priority relative to other
actions, because it never got past this earlier, completely separate gate.
The same is true of the STOCK "panic"/"outnumbered" triggers a few lines
above (ACTION_EMERGENCY + 9 = 99) - nothing in this codebase's built-in
emergency tier crosses 100, which is presumably why unwatched bots have
never reliably fled anything at all, independent of this patch.

THE FIX: the retreat trigger's relevance is now a literal 100.0f, not a
further offset from ACTION_EMERGENCY - clearing the minimal-mode floor is
the entire intent, and a flat 100 keeps that legible to the next reader
rather than reading as an arbitrary-looking number that happens to be big
enough. Deliberately NOT changing AllowActivity()/BotActiveAlone itself:
that governs update frequency for the whole 500-bot simulation and has real
performance implications server-wide; this fix is scoped to the one action
that needs to survive minimal mode, not the throttling system itself.

REBASED 2026-09-03 onto mod-playerbots master 2f7d9f77. Upstream removed
FleeAction's `distance` constructor parameter and its private member, which
were the lines this patch's MovementActions.h hunk anchored on, so the hunk
stopped applying. Only the anchor changed: RetreatFromDangerAction carries
its own distance seeded from fleeDistance and never referenced FleeAction's,
so the inserted code is byte-identical apart from a note recording this.
The other five files in this patch applied to upstream unchanged.

REBASED 2026-10-19 onto mod-playerbots @ 40884e9c135c1daf27ffd894a6047502a5701130
(quadseven/mod-playerbots branch rebase-2026-10-19: upstream master d750b4b3
plus the two fork-only fall-damage commits).

Upstream #2825 ("Clean up fleeing v1") deleted FleeStrategy and its
"flee" strategy name, which this patch used to load into the non-combat engine
and add its trigger to. The trigger now has a strategy of its own,
RetreatFromDangerStrategy ("retreat from danger", two new files registered in
StrategyContext), and AiFactory loads that instead. The old FleeStrategy's own
panic / outnumbered / critical-health "flee" triggers are NOT restored: upstream
removed them as inoperative, and out of combat they never had a target anyway.
RetreatFromDangerAction, the relevance of 100 and the GrindTargetValue attacker
filter are unchanged. Upstream #2854 renamed fleeDistance to FleeDistance.

